nodecode-godot is a rewrite of Tormen28's Tormen28/argos:

  https://github.com/Tormen28/argos
  commit ab194154310cf4a44cee932d7c33f7fefb42c95d
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Tormen28 (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the repository root of that commit: a plugin
manifest, an mcp.json naming a Python MCP server, the argos addon for the
Godot editor (addons/argos), and one skill (skills/argos/SKILL.md). The
rewrite keeps the editor side as it is -- the addon still runs inside the
user's Godot project, still publishes .argos/port and .argos/token, and still
answers {id, token, action, ...} with {ok, id, result|error, detail} on a
loopback WebSocket -- and replaces the Python MCP server with Lisp. Nodecode
reads the published port and token (or the ones the `godot` section pins),
opens the bridge once per call, sends one action, reads one answer, and closes
it. Every argos_* tool of the original has a Nodecode tool under a godot_*
name with the same arguments: ping, status, scene tree/open/save/reload/new,
node create/instantiate/reparent/get_prop/set_prop/delete, action_batch,
undo, redo, anim list/get/play/stop, run play/stop, capture (the 2D editor
viewport and a rendered scene), fs_scan and raw. The skill becomes the tool
descriptions, the (help :godot) page and /godot, which reports the bridge and
what the editor says of itself.

What was left out, and why: the original's process tools -- editor_launch,
editor_stop, editor_quit and check (which runs the Godot binary with
--check-only over .gd files) -- because they need a launcher and the Godot
executable rather than the addon's bridge, so the editor is started by the
user; the runtime tools (runtime_info and runtime_capture), which watch a
game in play through a second script rather than the addon's request/answer
wire; the operation history (history, .argos/history.json), which the Python
server writes; and the tool-permission prompts and ARGOS_PROJECT /
ARGOS_ALLOWED_PROJECTS allowlist of the MCP server, because a cell's tools are
the user's own and none of the verbs left here starts a project. LICENSE is
the repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
